= Agent memory (artificial intelligence) =

== Overview ==
In artificial intelligence, '''agent memory''' is persistent or selectively retained computational state that allows an artificial agent to use information from prior observations, interactions, actions, or learned experience in future decision-making.

The term covers multiple architectures rather than one storage mechanism.

== Why agents use memory ==
Memory can help an agent:

* maintain state across long tasks;
* remember prior interactions;
* avoid repeating failed actions;
* retain user or environment facts;
* reuse successful procedures;
* update beliefs after observations;
* coordinate across sessions or agents.

== Implementations ==
Agent memory can be implemented through:

* context-resident summaries;
* external databases;
* retrieval systems;
* key-value stores;
* logs or event histories;
* learned latent state;
* parametric updates;
* structured knowledge stores.

A vector database is one possible implementation, not the definition.

== Context window ==
A model's current context window is temporary input state.

Agent memory can persist beyond one context window or one model invocation.

Therefore:

<pre>
agent memory ≠ context window
</pre>

== Retrieval-augmented generation ==
Retrieval-augmented generation can retrieve stored information for a model.

It can be part of agent memory, but retrieval by itself does not define memory management.

Agent memory may also include writing, updating, consolidation, forgetting, conflict resolution, and policy-controlled recall.

== Human-memory analogy ==
Terms such as “working”, “episodic”, “semantic”, “short-term”, and “long-term” are often borrowed from cognitive science.

These analogies can be useful, but computational systems do not automatically implement the corresponding biological mechanisms.

== Related concepts ==
[[AI agent]], [[Memory]], [[Working memory]], [[Database]], [[Knowledge representation]], [[Provenance]], and [[Large language model]] clarify the computational/biological boundary.

[[Information]] provides the broader representational context: agent memory is a mechanism for retaining and recalling selected information across time, not a synonym for information itself.

